    "title": "THE GLASSWORKER REVIEW - by Edward Douglas",
    "text": "Courtesy Watermelon Pictures There definitely seems to be a rising demand for more hand-drawn animation among enthusiasts of the genre, and for better or worse, it seems to be happening more overseas than it is from within the studio system.\nThe Glassworker is the brainchild of filmmaker Usman Riaz, who wanted to get an animation studio set up in Karachi, Pakistan. According to an introduction in front of the movie, that meant “reverse-engineering” how some of the more popular animated films were made. Presumably, Riaz and his team watched a lot of films from Japan, including the movies of Studio Ghibli, since that’s ingrained into the DNA of The Glassworker . That’s merely to say that the storytelling and craft behind the movie are on par with those films, though the story itself is very much something that could only take place in Pakistan.\nCourtesy Watermelon Pictures First, we meet young Vincent Oliver and his widower father Tomas (voiced by Teresa Gallagher and Art Malik), living in Karachi, Pakistan and working at Tomas’ glassworks, a family business that creates the most gorgeous designs, with Vincent eager to take over for his father. But first, he meets Alliz (Anjli Mohindra), the daughter of a wealthy military leader Colonel Amano (Tony Jayawardena). She’s an accomplished violinist, and the two quickly become friends, though Vincent’s father does not approve of Alliz’s father, the military, or the war taking place. The film cuts between the older Vincent (voiced by Sacha Dhawan) to flashbacks of the younger Vincent spending time with Alliz, which is meant to inform how their relationship develops in the years in between.\nAt first, I was a little bummed that the version of the movie I was given to watch was the dubbed version, because in general, I prefer watching animation in the original language with subtitles. Unlike another dubbed animation movie coming out soon (actually next week), the English voice work was quite good and presumably done in-house at Mano Animation Studios. The dubbing was done well enough that it never felt like a distraction from the dialogue or storytelling.\nCourtesy Waternelon Pictures The story itself is quite fascinating, definitely paying homage to Studio Ghibli, but as far as Miyazaki goes, this might be closest to the master’s 2013 film, The Wind Rises , vs. any of his more fantasy-driven storytelling. In fact, The Glassworker is so grounded in reality that you’ll wonder things like when the movie takes place (I don’t believe a date was ever mentioned) and which war is taking place. But again, that’s secondary to following the relationship between Vincent and Alliz and how it evolves and runs into hurdles due to Tomas’ feelings about the war. It’s never a romantic relationship perse, but there are clearly feelings that get muddled with everything happening around them, as well as Vincent’s lower-class uneducated status that makes it hard to do things like attend a high-profile recital by Alliz that is clearly important for him to be there.\nCourtesy Watermelon Pictures The visuals are beautiful and well-crafted, but what makes the film truly special is the equally gorgeous score. Because music plays as much a part in the story as the glassblowing, it plays such a crucial role, with Riaz composing the music along with Carmine Di Florio. It’s always interesting to me when a filmmaker also provides the music, because the music is as important as the visuals, the story and dialogue in whether a movie works as a whole, and that’s especially the case here. I will mention that I felt similarly to the music in last year’s Oscar-winning Flow .\nThe Glassworker is an absolutely lovely animated film that might get a bit dark and violent in its last act to bring the youngest of kids, but as a movie that gives us a glimpse of life in Pakistan during this turbulent time with a specific focus on the arts, it’s quite an achievement and a joy to behold.\nRating: 8/10\nThe Glassworker will open in select cities on Friday, August 15.\n2\nShare this post\nThe Weekend Warrior Newsletter THE GLASSWORKER REVIEW\nCopy link\nFacebook\nEmail\nNotes More\nShare",
